1. THE CORE CLAIM (THE PAPER\'92S \'93ONE SENTENCE\'94)\
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Behavioral adoption is not adequately explained at the individual level alone:\
adoption rates are emergent outcomes of populations embedded in relational\
structure. When we replace plausible network structure (imputed from ego-network\
data and homophily) with random networks that preserve size and density (ER),\
adoption declines substantially, indicating that network topology is not a\
neutral carrier but a causal condition shaping the macro-level diffusion\
outcome.\
\
A compact version for abstract/introduction:\
\'93Adoption is not merely aggregated choice; it is structured emergence. Networks\
decide for us in the sense that realistic social topology systematically\
enables diffusion beyond what individual preferences or rational incentives\
predict.\'94\
\
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
2. WHY THIS IS SOCIOLOGICAL (NOT JUST A MODELING EXERCISE)\
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Your project is sociological in three ways:\
\
2.1 Macro outcomes from micro mechanisms (emergence)\
The dependent object of explanation is collective: final adoption levels,\
diffusion barriers, and phase transitions. These are properties of the social\
system, not reducible to a distribution of individual propensities. The model\
shows how the same individuals, with the same preference distribution, can\
produce radically different collective outcomes depending on the relational\
arrangement of ties.\
\
2.2 Structure is not a \'93control variable\'94; it is a generative condition\
A standard move in empirical social science is to treat networks as nuisance or\
as \'93context\'94 appended to individuals. Your results motivate the stronger claim:\
topology is constitutive of diffusion. Randomization of structure (ER rewiring)\
reduces adoption even when holding constant N and density, suggesting that\
clustering/community structure, path redundancy, and tie configuration matter.\
\
2.3 Social influence is selective and socially stratified\
Influence is not homogeneous: similarity biases influence, meaning contagion is\
conditioned by social distance and homophily. This is a sociological statement\
about stratification, boundaries, and selective exposure\'97operationalized in the\
model through tie-specific effectiveness (h threshold / maximum social\
proximity) and social distance (e.g., Gower distance on demographics).\
\
