5. THE DIFFUSION MODEL (WRITE THIS AS \'93THEORY-INFORMED MECHANISM\'94)\
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
This is the conceptual model section. It should read like sociological theory,\
not purely computational description.\
\
5.1 Actors are not identical carriers\
Individuals have:\
- a latent/constructed propensity to adopt (minimum utility requirement, q),\
- a position in a structured social environment (network ties),\
- exposure that is socially filtered (selective influence).\
\
5.2 Innovations/behaviors have intrinsic appeal\
The diffusion object (innovation or collective-action behavior) has an intrinsic\
utility level (IUL). High IUL means the behavior is attractive to many even\
without social reinforcement; low IUL means adoption requires social support.\
\
5.3 Two adoption channels\
Channel A: Rational choice adoption\
Adoption occurs if utility exceeds the actor\'92s requirement. This channel captures\
individual preference alignment and incentive compatibility.\
\
Channel B: Social adoption\
Adoption occurs if effective exposure exceeds threshold. This channel captures\
word-of-mouth contagion, social reinforcement, and threshold cascades.\
\
5.4 Selective social influence (homophily as a gate)\
Social influence is not simply \'93number of adopting neighbors.\'94\
It is:\
- filtered by social distance (multi-dimensional similarity),\
- modulated by MSP/h (how tolerant actors are of dissimilar influence).\
Thus, influence is stratified: peers who are \'93close\'94 count more (or exclusively),\
making diffusion contingent on homophilic ties and local structure.\
\
5.5 Why this matters sociologically\
This model encodes two central sociological intuitions:\
- influence is relational (not an individual trait),\
- similarity structures communication and credibility (social boundaries).\
Diffusion success depends not only on preferences but on whether the network\
creates reinforcement paths among similar actors.\
\
