6. WHAT THE REGRESSION RESULTS SAY (INTERPRETATION YOU CAN USE IN RESULTS)\
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Below is an interpretation that is sociological and aligned with your \'93networks\
decide\'94 conclusion. You can translate it into paper prose directly.\
\
6.1 Shared finding across models: network randomization depresses adoption\
In all three BAMs, network_typeER is negative, meaning ER networks reduce the\
log-odds of adoption relative to plausible networks, even though ER preserves N\
and density.\
\
- Rational adoption: network_typeER = -0.1139\
- Social adoption:   network_typeER = -0.4261\
- Total adoption:    network_typeER = -0.8500\
\
Interpretation:\
Randomizing structure undermines diffusion. The penalty is smallest for purely\
rational adoption (where network is only the carrier of exposure opportunities),\
larger for social adoption (where reinforcement and local clustering matter),\
and largest for total adoption (which accumulates both mechanisms and is most\
sensitive to whether social cascades can form).\
\
This supports the macro claim: topology is not incidental; it is a determinant of\
system-level adoption.\
\
6.2 Threshold structure is a major \'93systemic constraint\'94 (tau_mean and tau_sd)\
Across models, tau_mean is negative and extremely strong in social and total\
adoption, indicating that a population with higher resistance collapses\
diffusion.\
\
- Rational: tau_mean = -0.185 (negative but modest)\
- Social:   tau_mean = -5.130 (dominant)\
- Total:    tau_mean = -6.944 (dominant)\
\
tau_sd is positive and large for social and total adoption:\
- Social: tau_sd = +4.210\
- Total:  tau_sd = +5.942\
\
Interpretation:\
Heterogeneity in thresholds (tau_sd) creates \'93weak points\'94 that can seed cascades\
even when average resistance is high. Sociologically, this maps onto the idea\
that minority segments with low thresholds (early adopters, activists, highly\
susceptible groups) can unlock diffusion if the network provides reinforcement\
paths.\
\
6.3 Seeding strategy: centrality matters, but differently by mechanism\
Total adoption:\
- central/closeness/eigen are strongly positive (~ +0.118 to +0.120)\
- marginal is negative (-0.0668)\
\
Social adoption:\
- central/closeness/eigen are positive (~ +0.032)\
- marginal is negative (-0.0198)\
\
Rational adoption:\
- central/closeness/eigen are negative (~ -0.051 to -0.054)\
- marginal is positive (+0.0268)\
\
Interpretation:\
- For social diffusion, central seeding accelerates cascades because central\
  nodes sit on reinforcement-rich paths and can ignite large neighborhoods.\
- For rational diffusion, central seeding is slightly worse than random, and\
  marginal seeding slightly better, suggesting that \'93rational adoption\'94 is\
  governed more by coverage and reach into less redundant areas than by\
  clustering. Central nodes may be surrounded by similar nodes and redundant\
  exposure; marginal seeds may penetrate otherwise unexposed peripheries.\
- Total adoption inherits the social logic strongly (because once social\
  influence activates, it amplifies adoption far beyond purely rational uptake).\
\
6.4 The non-linear surfaces: f(IUL, MSP) are not minor details; they are the core\
All smooths s(IUL, MSP) are highly significant, with edf ~ 9 and massive Chi-sq,\
for both contagion types (Innovation and Collective Action). This means:\
- IUL and MSP interact nonlinearly,\
- diffusion feasibility regions emerge in the parameter space,\
- \'93phase transition-like\'94 boundaries are plausible and visible in surfaces.\
\
Interpretation for narrative:\
Rather than a linear tradeoff (\'93more utility \uc0\u8594  more adoption\'94), diffusion depends\
on an interaction between:\
- the intrinsic attractiveness of the behavior (IUL), and\
- the social permeability of influence (MSP).\
When MSP is low (narrow influence boundaries), diffusion can stall even for\
moderately attractive behaviors. When MSP is high, social reinforcement can\
overcome low intrinsic attractiveness, generating sudden shifts from low to high\
adoption\'97consistent with abrupt collective fashions and cascades.\
\
6.5 Deviance explained: why the mechanisms differ in predictability\
- Rational adoption: deviance explained ~ 0.969 (near-deterministic)\
- Social adoption:   deviance explained ~ 0.711 (substantial but noisier)\
- Total adoption:    deviance explained ~ 0.822 (high, combining both channels)\
\
Interpretation:\
Rational adoption is more predictable because it closely tracks utility and\
threshold parameters. Social adoption is inherently contingent on structure and\
local reinforcement; it depends on network configuration in ways not fully\
captured by global covariates. Total adoption, once social cascades are included,\
recovers predictability because the structural signature is strong and systematic\
(plausible vs ER difference is large).\
